<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">The Pillow Book</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1002">1002</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Makura no Sōshi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Pillow Book is a collection of observations, anecdotes, lists, poems and essays written by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sei-shonagon">Sei Shōnagon</a> while she was a lady at the court of Empress Consort Fujiwara no Teishi in Heian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, completed in 1002. Written for her own enjoyment and chiefly in hiragana, the script women then used while men typically wrote in Chinese, it mixes accounts of life at court, her opinions on many matters and 164 lists such as 'hateful things'. It is one of the best sources for the court society of the 10th century and a landmark of Japanese literature, which shaped the genre of zuihitsu, assorted writings. It circulated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> until it was first printed in the 17th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/the-pillow-book.jpg" alt="The Pillow Book" width="330" height="359" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 土佐光起 (Tosa Mitsuoki) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sei_Shonagon2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The book is a collection of essays, anecdotes, poems and descriptive passages with little connection to one another, except that they express the ideas and whims of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sei-shonagon">Sei Shōnagon</a>, prompted by moments of her daily life. Translator Meredith McKinney calls it a genre-bending miscellany of short, largely unrelated pieces. Its writing falls into three kinds: narratives of events she witnessed at court, her own thoughts and opinions on various matters, and the lists, of which there are 164, with headings such as hateful things, things of beauty and things that make the heart grow fonder.</p><p>Its paragraphs are brief and easy to read even for modern Japanese speakers. Shōnagon observes everyday life and the four seasons, and the word okashi, lovely, recurs in place of the pathos of things found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-tale-of-genji">The Tale of Genji</a>. Her feelings are mixed with subtle sentiment reflecting the downfall of Fujiwara no Michitaka, the father of Empress Teishi, and the misfortunes of the emperor and empress.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Shōnagon meant her writing to be private, as a way to express thoughts she could not state publicly because of her lower status at court. Part of it was accidentally revealed during her life: she left it on a cushion put out for a visiting guest, who carried it off despite her pleas.</p><p>In the late 10th and early 11th centuries Japanese men typically wrote in Chinese characters, while women wrote in hiragana, a syllabary derived from them, and the book is written entirely in Japanese. According to Matthew Penney, the only Chinese terms are in place-names and personal titles. Most of it was probably written while she served at court, though some later entries seem to be drawn from memory.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The book circulated at court and for several hundred years existed in handwritten <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, with scribes changing the order of entries and adding or removing passages. Four main variants are known, of which the Sankanbon and Nōinbon are held the most complete and accurate. It was first printed in the 17th century. Six passages were first translated into English in 1889 by T.</p><p>A. Purcell and W. G. Aston, followed by Arthur Waley's partial translation in 1928, Ivan Morris's complete one in 1967 and Meredith McKinney's in 2006. It influenced the zuihitsu genre, whose other major works include Kamo no Chōmei's Hōjōki and Yoshida Kenkō's Tsurezuregusa, and which found a wide audience among the merchant classes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “The Pillow Book” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
